<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Murbach</h1><p class="st-meta">727 CE – 1789 CE (approx.) · capital Guebwiller</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A wealthy Alsatian abbey whose abbots were princes of the Empire, ruling a small Vosges territory until the French Revolution swept it away.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Murbach Abbey was founded in 727 in a valley of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirmin">Saint Pirmin</a>, with the support of the Alsatian duke Eberhard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etichonids">Etichonid</a> family. It soon became one of the richest monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, with estates stretching into Swabia and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a>, including the town of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-lucerne">Lucerne</a>, which it held until 1291.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbots became princes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guebwiller">Guebwiller</a> and the surrounding valleys. Only nobles could join the community. In 1560 Murbach was united with the Abbey of Lure. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> in 1648, the princely abbots kept their rights under French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque</a> abbey church still stands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The monks, wearied by the remote valley, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guebwiller">Guebwiller</a>, and in 1764 the abbey became a secular collegiate chapter. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> in 1789 the chapter's lands were seized and its palaces in Guebwiller looted, ending more than a thousand years of ecclesiastical lordship.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-murbach.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Murbach" width="320" height="480"><figcaption>Image: Alex für die Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abtei_Murbach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>727 CE – 1789 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Guebwiller</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pirmin, Bartholomew of Andlau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Murbach Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1258&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-murbach" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Murbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4847 500 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Murbach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5167 756L5229 694M5167 756L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5167 756h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5167 756h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1258&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-murbach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

